title: Namespace-shortcut completion offers raw storage keys for a configured Nadeo module
updated_at: "2026-08-30T17:27:56Z"
---
Found 2026-08-31 while evaluating the B056 review. Not fixed by B056; the
review's `f7d99b7` carries a candidate fix that was held back as out of scope.

## Actual Behavior

`add_shortcut_completions` (`src/server/completion.rs`) iterates the user's
configured shortcut namespaces and offers `index.namespace_enums(ns)`, which
yields INDEX KEYS. Configure a synthetic Nadeo module such as `Game` as a
shortcut namespace and completion offers labels like:

    Game::CGameMenu::EMenuOrder

That is the loader's storage key, not a spelling the game accepts. Since B056
the resolver rejects it, so the LSP suggests a name and then flags it as
unknown - the tool contradicting itself.

## Expected Behavior

Offer the SOURCE spelling (`CGameMenu::EMenuOrder`), or offer nothing for a
namespace that is not a real source scope. `Game` is not a scope: the B052
corpus records `Game::ZZZNoSuchEnumName` -> `Namespace 'Game' doesn't exist.`

## Note on the obvious fix

Gating the whole loop on `index.scope_exists(ns)` also gates FUNCTION
shortcuts, which is wider than the enum defect. That widening is harmless -
every namespace owning free functions is necessarily a scope, because only
Core contributes free functions and `build_scope_index` credits every Core
function's namespace. `every_namespace_that_owns_functions_is_a_scope`
(`src/typedb/index.rs`) pins both halves and is red-checked, so the gate can
be placed with that fence already in place.

## Related

B056 (source names), B052 (scope rules), B016 (shortcut completions).